\section{The width carries the family}{


An IPv4 address encodes to \strong{4} octets, 8 hex digits or 32 bits; an IPv6
address to \strong{16} octets, 32 hex digits or 128 bits. The width is decided by
the family and never by the bits, which is what keeps \verb{::ffff:192.0.2.1}
apart from \verb{192.0.2.1}: the two share their low 32 bits (RFC 4291 §2.5.5) and
the length is the only thing that tells them apart.

The 4-in-6 form therefore encodes to the \strong{full 16 octets}, not to the 4 of
the address it embeds. Use \code{\link[=addr_embeddings]{addr_embeddings()}} when the embedded address is
what you want.
}

\section{Leading zeros}{


Every output is fixed width and zero padded. \verb{::1} is 32 hex digits, 31 of
them \code{0}. This is the opposite of RFC 5952 §4.1, which suppresses leading
zeros -- that rule is about \emph{text} form, and these are not text forms.

Decoding is exact about it: a string of any width other than the two the
family fixes decodes to \code{NA}, and is never padded to the nearest one. Seven
hex digits could be an IPv4 address missing a zero or an IPv6 address missing
twenty-five, and raddr will not guess.
}

\section{What survives a round trip, and what does not}{


\code{bytes_to_addr(addr_to_bytes(x))} \strong{equals} \code{x}, and likewise for the other
two pairs. Equality is over the 128 bits and the family (see
\code{\link[=raddr_address]{raddr_address()}}), and all three encodings preserve both.

The \strong{zone does not survive}. \verb{fe80::1\%eth0} and \verb{fe80::1\%eth1} encode to
identical octets, and the decoders return an address with no zone at all --
RFC 4007 §6 explains why it cannot be recovered, since zone indices are
strictly local to a node. The round trip still satisfies \code{==} because the
zone does not participate in equality, but \code{\link[=addr_zone]{addr_zone()}} on the result is
\code{NA}. Carry it separately if you need it.

A \strong{prefix length} does not survive either, for the simpler reason that an
address does not carry one. Four octets are \verb{192.0.2.0}, full stop.
}

\section{Case, prefixes and grouping on input}{


Hex output is lowercase, following RFC 5952 §4.3. Uppercase input is
accepted, because RFC 3596 §2.5 and RFC 2874 §2.2.1 both print their examples
in uppercase.

\code{hex_to_addr()} accepts an optional \verb{0x} or \verb{0X} prefix and never emits one.
No RFC defines a \verb{0x}-prefixed address encoding; it is a presentation
convention, so raddr reads it and does not write it.

Whitespace grouping -- \verb{c000 0201}, or a binary string spaced per octet -- is
stripped on input by both string decoders. Nothing else is normalised away.
}

